.TH FETCHVER 1 "2026-07-30" "Selever" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
fetchver \- resolve a version query to an exact version
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fetchver node
.I version
.br
.B fetchver npm
.I package[@version]
.br
.B fetchver gopkg
.I package[@version]
.br
.B fetchver dotnet
.I package[@version]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fetchver
resolves a version prefix, tag, codename, or package query and writes the
matching exact version to standard output.
It is intended for commands such as
.BR selever (1)
that require exact versions.
.PP
The
.B node
command prints a version number.
Package commands print the package name followed by
.B @
and the resolved version.
Resolution selects stable releases unless the underlying Go module query
explicitly selects another version.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "node " version
Resolve a Node.js release.
.I version
may be
.BR latest ,
a one-, two-, or three-part numeric prefix, or an LTS codename.
A leading
.B v
is accepted for numeric versions.
LTS codenames are matched without regard to case.
.TP
.BI "npm " package[@version]
Resolve an npm package from its configured registry.
The optional version may be a one-, two-, or three-part numeric prefix or a
dist-tag.
Omitting it is equivalent to
.BR @latest .
.TP
.BI "gopkg " package[@version]
Resolve a Go module by running
.BR "go list \-m \-json" .
The optional version is a Go module version query.
Omitting it is equivalent to
.BR @latest .
A numeric query is passed to Go with a leading
.BR v .
.TP
.BI "dotnet " package[@version]
Resolve a dotnet tool package through NuGet.
The optional version may be
.B latest
or a one-, two-, or three-part stable version prefix.
Omitting it is equivalent to
.BR @latest .
Package names are matched without regard to case, and the registry's spelling
is used in the result.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show help.
.SH OUTPUT
One resolved value is written to standard output followed by a newline.
.PP
.EX
$ fetchver node 22
22.23.1
$ fetchver npm typescript@5
typescript@5.9.3
$ fetchver gopkg golang.org/x/tools/gopls@0.14
golang.org/x/tools/gopls@v0.14.2
.EE
.SH NPM CONFIGURATION
The npm resolver reads the user's npm configuration and then
.I .npmrc
in the current directory.
The user configuration path is selected by
.BR npm_config_userconfig ,
.BR NPM_CONFIG_USERCONFIG ,
or
.I $HOME/.npmrc
in that order.
Settings from the current directory override user settings.
.PP
The registry is selected from a scoped
.B @scope:registry
setting, the
.B npm_config_registry
or
.B NPM_CONFIG_REGISTRY
environment variable, the
.B registry
setting, or
.I https://registry.npmjs.org
in that order.
An
.B _authToken
setting for the selected registry is sent as a bearer token.
Environment variables in npm configuration values are expanded.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOME
Locates the default user npm configuration.
.TP
.B npm_config_userconfig, NPM_CONFIG_USERCONFIG
Select the user npm configuration file.
.TP
.B npm_config_registry, NPM_CONFIG_REGISTRY
Override the default npm registry.
.TP
.B GOPROXY
Controls Go module resolution performed by
.BR "go list" .
Other Go environment variables used by that command also apply.
.TP
.B HTTP_PROXY, HTTPS_PROXY, NO_PROXY
Control proxies for Node.js, npm, and NuGet HTTP requests.
.TP
.B PATH
Used to locate
.B go
for
.BR "fetchver gopkg" .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $HOME/.npmrc
Default user npm configuration.
.TP
.I ./.npmrc
Project npm configuration.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B fetchver
exits with status 0 after writing a match.
It exits non-zero when the query is invalid, no stable release matches, package
metadata cannot be retrieved, or the Go command fails.
.SH EXAMPLES
Resolve the current Node.js release for an LTS line:
.PP
.EX
fetchver node Hydrogen
.EE
.PP
Install the latest matching Node.js 22 release:
.PP
.EX
selever node "$(fetchver node 22)"
.EE
.PP
Resolve an omitted package version:
.PP
.EX
fetchver dotnet dotnet-format
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR selever (1),
.BR globver (1),
.BR go-list (1),
.BR npmrc (5)
